  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 228 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nA longwave trough will amplify over the eastern CONUS by Thursday \nestablishing cyclonic flow across the Mid-Mississippi River Valley. \nIn this flow, multiple shortwave troughs/vorticity maxima will pass \noverhead with the most potent shortwave trough passing on Thursday, \nfavoring sporadic light showers/sprinkles and/or drizzle along/east \nof the Mississippi River among extensive stratocumulus. These clouds \nand reinforced CAA will result in high temperatures in the 40s to \nlow-50s F on Thursday. However, the core of this seasonably cool \nairmass will arrive Friday into Saturday with the coldest \ntemperatures we have seen so far this season including highs in the \n40s F and lows in the 20s F aided by an accompanying nearby surface \nanticyclone.\n\nGlobal model guidance remains in agreement that mid-level heights \nwill begin rising over the Mid-Mississippi River Valley Saturday and \nSunday as the longwave trough in the eastern CONUS progresses \neastward. The NBM temperature distribution contains an underlying \nwarming trend into next week, but the interquartile range inflates \nto around 10 F owing to differences in how quickly the surface \nanticyclone retreats and low-level flow can become more southerly. \nAnother point of uncertainty is the potential for a weakening upper-\nlevel shortwave trough to propagate across the Central Plains into \nthe Mid or Lower Mississippi River Valley late Sunday into Monday. \nIt is not yet clear if this feature would provide any rain to the \nCWA with the highest probabilities of measurable rainfall from \nglobal ensemble model guidance currently to the west and southwest \nbut there could at least be increased cloud cover.",
